<h2>Common Grand Prairie claims</h2>
<h3>Large hail on homes and vehicles</h3>
<p>Hail of 2.75 to 3 inches can puncture shingles, crack skylights, break windows, and dent siding, gutters, and garage doors. Many Grand Prairie homes have brick veneer with composition shingle roofs, while newer homes may add stone, fiber-cement siding, and more complex roof lines. Our <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> page covers what to document.</p>
<h3>Wind and tornado damage</h3>
<p>High winds lift shingles, drop trees, and flatten fences, and brief tornadoes can damage a whole street at once. A complete scope includes the roof, exterior, other structures, and interior water damage from openings. See <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/tornado-damage-claims/">tornado damage claims</a>.</p>
<h3>Warehouses and distribution facilities</h3>
<p>Large low-slope roofs, metal panels, and rooftop equipment can carry significant hail and wind damage that is hard to see from the ground. These claims often involve many roof sections, drains, and flashing details. Learn more about <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a>.</p>
<h3>Flooding</h3>
<p>Flash flooding near creeks and low streets is generally excluded from homeowners and commercial policies and covered instead by NFIP or private flood policies. Water entering through storm damage may be handled differently; see <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>.</p>

